> As a Magicbane backstab is up to +d30, even a silver saber barely beats 
> this and only for a few targets, almost none of which will be on Astral.

Yes, we all know you're fond of "magicbane + backstab".

This doesn't address the issue of whether Rogues can #twoweapon effectively,
though, as wielding Magicbane doesn't preclude #twoweapon (though it is,
indeed, more difficult to backstab while #twoweaponing).

Note that 'effectively' does not mean 'is best in all cases'.  You
concede this above when you mention that a silver saber can, in fact,
do more damage v. silver-haters... of which there are rather a large
number in the game.

> A shield of reflection frees up the amulet slot for power/life plus 
> allows the use of a wider dragon scale selection.

While true, this doesn't really address the original question; it's more
of a comparison of the new options available with regard to DSM,
amulets, and shield usage.  All classes who can #twoweapon must choose
between shields and #twoweaponing, of course; this is hardly specific to
Rogues.

> It's a pity because spork rogues now start with knife and dagger skills 
> and Crysknife does more damage to everything on Astral than the saber. 

There are, indeed, few silver-haters on Astral, so #twoweaponing a
crysknife there would seem to be quite effective.  But this is _in
favor_ of #twoweapon, not against it.

All things considered, I still can't see why a hefty precondition of
"early-game artifact wish" (and it must be a wish, as Magicbane is
neutral and Rogues are chaotic) should convince me that #twoweapon isn't
an effective choice for Rogues, and might even be the best choice in
some circumstances.

For example, why not use Magicbane + backstab up to Gehennom, switch
over to (artifact) + silver saber and #twoweapon through Hell, and then swap 
out the saber for a crysknife when you hit the End Game?

